ith party key over contract signing bytes. False or unusable refuses, even if same party has good signature later. So we check all signatures, if any bad, fail. Need party key from parties by domain.\n- Rule 16: each party has at least one signature that verifies.\n\nWe'll implement a function verifies_signature(pub_b64, sig_b64, msg) returning bool or unusable. We'll treat unusable as false for refusal.\n\nFor public key validation, we need to decode and check point. We'll implement `decode_public_key(pub_b64)` returning point or raises.\n\nWhen verifying, we need to handle strictness? The door just needs verify (3.4) which returns true/false/unusable; strictness not relevant here. So we use cryptography's verify for Ed25519. But we must also check key validity per 3.2 (point checks) before using. So we'll implement `verify_ed25519(pub_bytes, sig_bytes, message)` that first validates public key, then attempts verification using cryptography. If signature length !=64, unusable. If invalid, false.\n\nNote: Cryptography's `Ed25519PublicKey.from_public_bytes(pub_bytes).verify(sig, msg)` will raise InvalidSignature if fails. We'll catch and return false.\n\n4.2 Settleable terms: only if 4.1 accepts. We'll implement checks adding reasons.\n\n4.3 Strict terms: always run. Checks:\n- contract_signature_not_strict: for each signatures entry, if sig decodes to 64 bytes and not strict (S < L and R point valid). If any non-strict, add reason.\n- bond_malformed: if bond present not null, and not object with amount integer 1..2^53-1. Add reason.\n- parent_not_supplied: if parent_contract present not null and parent not supplied. Add reason.\n- parent_narrowing_failed: if parent_contract present not null, and parent provided but fails 4.3.1. Add reason.\n\n4.3.1 Parent narrowing (grant_subset): check many conditions between child grant and parent grant. Need to follow exactly. We'll implement.\n\nView verification (5.1) and 5.2 target decoding.\n\nWe'll have a Bitcoin header verification for a list of headers. We'll parse raw bytes from hex. Check height integers, hex length 160 (or 161 with newline? rule 9 handles strictness separately; for rules 1-8 we only need 160 hex, but if newline included, the hex string length >160 maybe. Actually rule 4 says each header hex 160 hex chars optionally followed by one line feed. In rule 1-8, we treat hex as 160 chars? We can strip trailing '\\n' for verification? Rule 4 says optionally followed by one line feed. So for chain verification, we can accept if after removing one trailing '\\n' it's 160 hex. We'll implement that for rules 1-8.\n- Rule 1: grant.finality.max_target_bits must decode to target; store floor target.\n- Rule 2: lower_bound object with integer height and 64 lower hex hash.\n- Rule 3: headers non-empty.\n- Rule 4: each header object with integer height >=0 and hex (after possibly stripping one newline) 160 hex, compute raw bytes.\n- Rule 5: heights consecutive.\n- Rule 6: from second header, prev hash matches bytes 4..36 reversed.\n- Rule 7: nBits decode, target <= floor, header hash <= target.\n- Rule 8: view holds header at lower_bound.height with hash equal to lower_bound.hash.\nIf any fails, add chain_view_rejected.\n\nRule 9: strictness of view: if view object with headers list, every header object with integer height and hex exactly 160 lower hex (no newline), and every string/key/number strict. If not, add view_not_strict. This is separate and does not stop walk.\n\nIf view verifies but >200000 headers, add input_too_large instead of chain_view_rejected? Spec: \"If the view verifies but holds more than 200000 headers, the reason is input_too_large instead, and no record is examined.\" So if rules 1-8 pass and headers count >200000, add input_too_large, and skip record examination. Need careful: We'll check after rules 1-8: if no chain_view_rejected and headers >200000, add input_too_large and mark skip_records.\n\nNow record handling flow:\n\nWe'll have a list of events (parsed from files). We need to produce for each event:\n- record_sha256 (if computable)\n- binding class (foreign, inconsistent, unbound, bound, dropped silently)\n- triage result (kept, set aside with reason, rejected, etc.)\n- for kept records: collapse, conformance, anchors, event_set.\n\nWe'll implement step by step in a single pass to manage state.\n\nState variables:\n- events: list\n- contract: object or None\n- csha: contract_sha256 or None\n- cid: contract.contract_id if object\n- pdg: contract.task.payload_digest (if present) else None\n- records_by_sha: dict to deduplicate identical bytes (record_sha256). We'll store first occurrence.\n- records: list of record objects that are bound and pass initial triage? We'll maintain a list of all records after binding and before triage.\n\nSince we might skip triage if contract/view failure, we need to still assign dispositions for binding classes. We'll have a function `classify_binding(record)` that returns (class, reason) and maybe store in dispositions.\n\nThe spec says \"Skip a record whose record_sha256 was already seen (identical bytes are one record).\" So we need to compute record_sha256 for each event. This requires canonical(record) which may fail for non-finite numbers? Actually canonical of any JSON value works; but if integer token too long, we may have parsed but not know; our parser will reject long integers entirely, so no issue. However, if numbers are float NaN etc, canonical can compute. So record_sha256 should be computable for all object records. For non-object records? The spec says \"Skip anything that is not an object, or whose schema is not one of three\". So we don't compute sha for those; no disposition.\n\nSo we loop over events in order:\n- if not object: skip.\n- if schema not one of three: skip.\n- compute record_sha256 (hex of sha256 of canonical(record)).\n- if sha already seen: skip (no new record).\n- else compute binding class using 6.1 rules.\n- store record info: record, sha, binding_class.\n- If class is \"bound\", add to bound_records list; else add to dispositions mapping with class.\n- Foreign/inconsistent: no triage.\n- Unbound: not settled in strict mode, but no triage? Spec says \"Only bound records go further. Foreign, inconsistent and unbound records are listed (section 9) and never settled.\" So unbound gets disposition \"unbound\" but no further processing.\n- Bound: will go further if we decide to process records (i.e., no skip due to contract/view).\n\nNote: \"not ours\" dropped silently, no disposition. That's when contract_ref absent/null and ref.contract_id != cid. So class \"not ours\" => no disposition; we just ignore.\n\nAfter binding, we have a list of bound records (with sha). If skip_records due to contract/view/large input/input_not_settleable etc, we don't go further; but we still have dispositions for foreign/inconsistent/unbound. Bound records get no disposition because unexamined.\n\nIf we proceed to triage (6.0), we need to process each bound record.\n\nTriage table (6.0). For each bound record:\n- Compute body: record without signatures and anchor.\n- Compute body strictness: as per strict definition, including specific checks (nenrin_ref, contract_ref fields, performed_actions type, delegated_to type, approvals list and sig strictness). The spec defines \"body is not strict when ...\" We'll implement `is_body_strict(record)`.\n- Compute anchor strictness (if anchor present).\n- Determine strictly signed: need one signature entry with allowed role and strictly verifies with matching key and is itself strict value. We'll implement `is_strictly_signed(record)` returning bool.\n- Then apply table in order:\n  - if not strictly signed and body not strict: set aside, reason record_not_strict? Actually table first row: \"not strictly signed, body not strict | set aside, record_not_strict\". So set aside, disposition \"record_not_strict\", no reason added? Wait \"The first three set aside or reject without one\". The first row says set aside, record_not_strict but no underspecified reason? The text: \"Only the fourth row both sets a record aside and adds a reason; the first three set aside or reject without one\". So first three do not add to underspecified_reasons. So we just set disposition accordingly (record_not_strict or signature_not_strict or rejected) but no reason.\n  - second: not strictly signed but signature verifies under 3.4 alone (lax) -> set aside, signature_not_strict, no reason.\n  - third: not strictly signed otherwise -> rejected, no reason.\n  - fourth: strictly signed, body not strict -> set aside, record_not_strict AND add reason record_not_strict.\n  - fifth: strictly signed, anchor not strict, and another bound record with same record signing bytes kept and anchors validly -> set aside, anchor_not_strict (no reason? The table says \"set aside, anchor_not_strict\" and this is one of first three? Actually the first three are rows 1-3; rows 4-6 are different. Row 5 says set aside, anchor_not_strict, but does it add reason? The paragraph says: \"Only the fourth row both sets a record aside and adds a reason; the first three set aside or reject without one\". So row 5 doesn't add reason? But the disposition is anchor_not_strict. However, row 6: strictly signed, anchor not strict, otherwise -> underspecified reason anchor_not_strict. So row 5 is when there is another valid copy with same signing bytes, so no reason; row 6 when no other valid copy, add reason. We'll implement accordingly.\n  - seventh: revocation and grant.revocation not object -> set aside, revocation_not_provided_for, no reason.\n  - else kept.\n\nWe need to track for each record: signed_body_bytes = record signing bytes (canonical of body? Actually record signing bytes are context + canonical(record without signatures and anchor). That's body canonical plus schema-specific prefix. We'll compute that for grouping later. However, for strict signed check we need verify signature over those bytes. We'll compute record_signing_bytes = context + canonical(body) (where body = record without signatures and anchor). Note: body includes contract_ref etc.\n\nWe'll store for each bound record: record_sha, record, body_is_strict, anchor_is_strict, strictly_signed, signed_body_bytes, maybe signatures info.\n\nAfter triage, we keep only records with result \"kept\". We'll have a list `kept_records`.\n\nSize limit: if len(kept_records) > 10000? Actually \"If more than 10000 records are kept by 6.0, add input_too_large and no record examined further.\" So if count > 10000, add reason input_too_large and skip further processing. We should add to underspecified_reasons; and no further examination. But we already have dispositions for triaged records (the set aside/rejected). Those remain. Bound records that were kept but not examined further? They get no disposition because unexamined? The spec says \"no record is examined further.\" So we stop before collapse/conformance/anchor. We'll not run those steps. The kept_records list will not get further dispositions.\n\nIf not too large, proceed to collapse (6.4) but only if section 5 rules 1-8 passed. Actually collapse is done when section 5 rules 1-8 passed, whether or not section 4 did. So we need to know if chain_view_rejected was added. If yes, skip collapse. If no, perform collapse.\n\nCollapse: group kept records by record_signing_bytes. For each group with more than one distinct record_sha256:\n- Determine which copies anchor validly (if any). Anchor validly: anchor.height integer >=0 that view holds; block_hash equals view hash; proof valid; batch leg accepts.\n- If at least one valid, keep the one with smallest (height, record_sha256) and drop others as duplicate_dropped.\n- If none valid, keep all.\n\nThis needed view information: heights, hashes, header merkle roots, and proof verification. We'll have view data structure.\n\nAfter collapse, we have final kept records.\n\nThen conformance check (6.6). For each kept record, check conformance problems as described. If execution with problem and contractor signature verifies, it's a charge: add deviation nonconforming_record and continue to section 7. Otherwise reject (disposition rejected) and remove from further processing. The computation is repeated until no new rejections, because a rejected record might affect others? Probably not, but we can just process sequentially and if we reject some, we don't need to reevaluate others because conformance is based on record itself. So no loop needed; just mark rejected.\n\nAfter conformance, we have list of records that passed (or charged but continue). For charged executions, they still go to anchors? Spec: \"If an execution with a problem has a contractor signature that verifies, it is a charge: the deviation nonconforming_record is added, and the record continues to section 7. Its acts are still judged.\" So yes, they proceed to anchors.\n\nNow anchors (section 7):\nFor each record that passed conformance (and not rejected), apply 7.4, 7.5, 7.6 in order. Based on results, assign disposition or accept.\n\nWe need height, block_hash, proof verification, batch leg check.\n- First, check height: anchor object and height integer >=0. If not, reason anchor_incomplete.\n- Then block_hash check: must be 64 lower hex. If not, anchor_incomplete.\n- Then height < lower_bound.height: anchor_below_lower_bound (add reason).\n- Then height > tip or not in view: anchor_beyond_observed_chain (add reason).\n- Then block_hash != view hash at height: disposition orphaned, excluded, no reason, but count for status.\n- Then proof: if fails, anchor_proof_invalid (add reason).\n- Then batch leg rule: if refuses, anchor_batch_leg_refused (add reason).\nIf all pass, accepted (disposition accepted, add to event_set list).\n\nWe need to compute body digest: sha256 of canonical(record without anchor). The anchor proof starts from these 32 bytes.\n\nProof verification (7.5): apply ops, current bytes length limit, final equals merkle root at height. Need to get merkle root from header raw bytes: bytes 36..68, not reversed.\n\nBatch leg rule (7.6): complex, we'll implement as described.\n\nAfter anchors, we have event_set list of accepted records.\n\nEvent set order: sort by (height, record_sha256).\n\nStatus (8.5): after verdict determined, but we can compute once we know final verdict and accepted/orphaned heights. If verdict underspecified, status undetermined. Else final if all accepted and orphaned heights <= horizon, else provisional.\n\nNow grant evaluation (section 10):\nWe need to walk event_set. This is independent of rejection etc.\nWe'll first process revocations to compute effective heights.\n- For each accepted revocation at height h, compute effective height:\n  - if effective_at anchor: E=h.\n  - if delivery_ack: E = min(h + ack_window, heights of accepted acknowledgements that name this revocation).\n    - Need to check naming: acknowledgement's revocation_sha256 equals lowercase hex body digest of revocation, or record_sha256 of a copy of that revocation that is still in input after 6.4. That's tricky: after 6.4, some copies may have been dropped. We need to know which record_sha256s of copies of that revocation were dropped? Actually we only have final kept records after collapse; but we can compute per revocation record its body digest, and also any other copies with same signed body but different anchor? The naming condition: equal to body digest of revocation (lowercase hex) or record_sha256 of a copy of that revocation that is still in input after 6.4. After collapse, we have a set of record_sha256s for records that were kept (including all copies if none anchored validly). But if some copies were dropped as duplicate_dropped, they are no longer in input after 6.4? The spec says \"A copy 6.4 dropped no longer names the revocation\". So for naming, we consider only record_sha256s of revocation copies that are still in final kept set (i.e., not duplicate_dropped). Also the body digest of revocation itself (maybe from any copy). We can compute body digest for each revocation record. So we'll have mapping from revocation record_sha to its body digest hex. For each accepted revocation, we compute its effective height based on acknowledgements.\n- Then sort revocations by (E, record_sha256).\n- For each execution, check if any revocation has E <= h; if so, all performed_actions give deviation revoked; skip other checks.\n\nThen for each accepted unrevoked execution:\n- Check expiry (10.2): if expiry_height present and not null, for each action if h > expiry_height, add after_expiry.\n- Then for each action: apply prohibited/conditional/unauthorized.\n- Then delegated_to entries not in delegation.allowed: add delegation.\n- Approvals classification: every approval in every accepted execution (revoked or not) is classified; forged approvals add forged_approval deviation. Counted approvals used for conditional actions only if not revoked? Actually conditionals are only applied to unrevoked executions (since revoked executions skip other checks). But approvals classification still runs for all accepted executions, even revoked, because forged approvals add deviation. So we process approvals globally after walk? We'll process approvals in the order they appear in event_set (record order and list order inside record). We'll maintain consumed_nonces set. For conditional actions, we need to find approvals from same record or earlier records (strictly below height). So we need to evaluate actions in order per execution, and approvals might be in same record; approvals in list order within record. The spec says the first approval that satisfies all four is used, taken in event set order (records by 8.1, approvals in list order within a record). So we'll iterate event_set in sorted order; for each record, we process its actions (if not revoked) and also classify its approvals, but approvals can be used for conditional actions in the same record or later? Actually conditional action at height h can use approval from same record s or from record at height strictly below h. So if an approval appears in same record as the action, it can be used. So we need to process actions and approvals in order within the record, but approvals that appear after the action in the same record still can be used? The spec says \"in event set order (records by 8.1, approvals in list order within a record)\". So when evaluating a conditional action, we should have already classified approvals that appear before it in list order? But the approval could be later in the same record, and still same record s and height h (same record's height). The condition says \"it is carried by the same record s, or by a record at a height strictly below h\". Since same record has same height h, an approval anywhere in that record satisfies the \"same record\" condition, regardless of order. So we can classify all approvals in a record before processing its actions, but the ordering of consumption matters if multiple conditional actions need same approval? Approval is single-use; if consumed, later actions cannot use. So we need to process actions in order, and for each conditional action, search through previously seen approvals (from earlier records and all approvals in current record? But if we haven't classified all approvals in current record yet, we might miss a usable approval that appears later in list. However, the approval appears in same record, so it should be available. To follow exact \"first approval that satisfies all four\" across entire event set in order, we should maintain a list of all classified approvals from records processed so far, and also include all approvals from the current record regardless of position. But then the \"first\" across the record would consider order of approvals in list; that's fine if we iterate approvals in list order when building the usable list. But we also need to ensure that approvals from later records (higher height) are not considered for actions in current record. So we can do two-pass: For each record, we first process its actions (using approvals from earlier records and from this record) and consume nonces; then after actions, we classify this record's approvals and add them to the pool for future records. But an approval in the same record could be used by an action in that record, which would happen before it's added to pool. That would be incorrect; we need to include same-record approvals. So we should, for each record, before processing its actions, classify its approvals and add them to a temporary per-record list, then evaluate actions with pooled approvals from earlier records plus this record's approvals in list order, but only those approvals that are from records with height <= h (which all earlier and same record satisfy). That works. After processing actions, we add the record's approvals to global pool (but careful: approvals consumed by conditional actions might be single-use; we need to mark consumed if used). We'll maintain global list of all classified approvals with their record height and sha, and consumed status. For each record in order, we first classify its approvals and append to a list `approvals_in_record`. Then for each action in that record, if conditional, we iterate over combined list of all approvals from earlier records (already in global_pool) plus `approvals_in_record` (in the order they appear in record) and find first that satisfies: action equal, (same record sha or earlier height < h? Actually earlier records have height < h, so height < h), h <= valid_until_height, nonce not consumed. If found, use it; if single_use, mark consumed (for global maybe). After processing actions, we add `approvals_in_record` to global_pool (with consumed flags already set maybe). But if an approval is consumed, we mark it consumed in the pool. We must ensure that approvals from same record are not considered for later actions in the same record if they were consumed earlier? That's fine because we mark consumed. Also, for actions in the same record, an approval that appears later in list could be used by an earlier action; that's allowed. So we need to have all approvals in record available before processing actions. So we'll first collect and classify approvals for the record, then process actions, then add approvals to global pool for future records.\n\nWe'll implement this carefully.\n\nNow NENRIN checks (section 11):\n- Cross check: if nenrin_records non-empty list, index walks by record_sha256. For each execution kept by 6.0 and not rejected by 6.6, left after 6.4 (once per record_sha256), regardless of anchor result (even orphaned), look up walk by execution's nenrin_ref. If found and walk's context.contract_sha256 present not null and != csha, add deviation evidence_names_other_contract. If nenrin_ref names a walk that was left out for not strict, add reason walk_not_strict.\n- Required evidence: if requirements.evidence==\"nenrin_required\", each accepted execution whose nenrin_ref not 64 hex gives deviation evidence_missing (after verdict found within_grant or deviation? \"This is applied after section 12 found within_grant or deviation.\" We'll compute deviations first, then if no underspecified and deviations or within_grant, check evidence_missing.)\n\nFinally, verdict:\n- If any underspecified_reasons non-empty: verdict = underspecified, deviations empty.\n- Else if any deviation_codes non-empty: verdict = deviation.\n- Else if event_set empty: verdict = nothing_to_settle.\n- Else within_grant.\n\nBut we need to compute deviations from all sections (10, 11, 6.6). We'll collect deviation_codes as we go.\n\nAfter verdict, compute status and bond_outcome.\n\nNow output JSON with fields.\n\nNeed to sort sets for output (sorted).\n\nNow implement all utilities: canonical, sha256, base64 canonical, public key validation, signature verification.\n\nLet's start coding.\n\nWe'll write the program with many helper functions.\n\nGiven the size, I'll produce complete code with careful comments.\n\nI'll structure:\n\n- imports: json (maybe not used if custom parser), hashlib, base64, binascii, re, sys, os, urllib.parse, struct, math, cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.ed25519 as ed25519, cryptography.exceptions.\n\n- Custom JSON parser class.\n\nFor canonicalization, we'll need to output non-ASCII strings without escaping (except control chars). We'll implement manual string escaping.\n\nWe'll write `to_canonical(obj)`.\n\nFor base64 canonical, use base64.b64decode(validate=True), re-encode.\n\nFor public key validation:\n\n- Decode bytes to point: parse sign bit and y. Implement modular exponent etc.\n\nWe'll implement Edwards point addition using extended coordinates? Simpler: use affine formulas, but addition and doubling with inversions mod p. Since we only need scalar multiplication by L to check subgroup, and L is ~2^252, that's 252 doublings and additions, each with modular inverse? That would be slow but maybe acceptable (few keys). We can implement using projective coordinates to avoid inversions per step. Use extended homogeneous coordinates? For simplicity, we can implement point addition in extended twisted Edwards coordinates using formulas from RFC8032, but that's complex. Alternatively, we can check subgroup by verifying that the point, when decoded, has order dividing L; we can use the fact that Ed25519 public keys in OpenSSL are validated for subgroup? Actually cryptography's `Ed25519PublicKey.from_public_bytes` might raise ValueError for invalid points? But does it check subgroup? I think it does not; it just stores the bytes. Verification will fail if using small order point because signature verification will always fail? Actually Ed25519 verification includes check that public key is in subgroup? Not sure. Better to implement the check.\n\nWe can implement point multiplication using extended coordinates with precomputed tables? That's heavy. Maybe we can use Python's `cryptography` to check by attempting to verify a signature with known message? Not helpful.\n\nGiven time, we can implement a simple affine addition using modular inverses; for L ~2^252, that's 252 iterations, each requiring a modular inverse; each inverse via pow(x, p-2, p) which is expensive (~O(log p) multiplications). That's ~252*255 ≈ 64260 big integer multiplications, which is okay maybe a few seconds per key. We'll have at most a handful of keys. Acceptable.\n\nWe'll implement affine Edwards addition formulas for twisted Edwards curve: -x^2 + y^2 = 1 + d*x^2*y^2.\n\nI'll use formulas from Wikipedia:\nAddition:\nx3 = (x1*y2 + y1*x2) / (1 + d*x1*x2*y1*y2)\ny3 = (y1*y2 + x1*x2) / (1 - d*x1*x2*y1*y2) with sign? Actually for twisted Edwards with a=-1:\nx3 = (x1*y2 + y1*x2) / (1 + d*x1*x2*y1*y2)\ny3 = (y1*y2 + x1*x2) / (1 - d*x1*x2*y1*y2)\nNeed to ensure denominators not zero.\n\nDoubling:\nx3 = (2*x1*y1) / (x1^2 + y1^2)\ny3 = (y1^2 - x1^2) / (2 - x1^2 - y1^2) with sign? Actually from same formulas with x1=x2: \nx3 = (2*x1*y1) / (1 + d*x1^2*y1^2)\ny3 = (y1^2 + x1^2) / (1 - d*x1^2*y1^2). But denominator may be zero if point is identity? We'll handle.\n\nIdentity is (0,1).\n\nWe'll compute scalar multiplication by L: binary expansion of L, double-and-add.\n\nWe'll implement modular inverse using pow(x, p-2, p).\n\nAfter computing [L]P, check == (0,1) identity. If yes, point is in prime-order subgroup.\n\nAlso need to check point is not identity: (0,1). And decoding formula.\n\nDecoding compressed point (32 bytes):\n- Interpret first 255 bits as y (little-endian? Actually in Ed25519 compressed format, the first 255 bits is the y coordinate, with the least significant bit of the last byte as the sign of x? Wait spec: The encoding of a point is 32 bytes: the first 255 bits are the y-coordinate in little-endian, and the most significant bit of the last byte is the sign of x. So we take bytes, clear the top bit to get y. Compute x from curve equation: x = ± sqrt( (y^2 - 1) / (d*y^2 + 1) ). The sign bit of the last byte indicates which x (0 for even, 1 for odd? Actually for compressed Edwards, the sign bit is x's least significant bit? We need to follow RFC8032 for Ed25519 point decompression: Given y and sign bit x_0, compute x = sqrt(...) with x_0 matching. Let's implement.\n\nWe'll follow RFC8032 section 5.1.3.\n\nNow canonical JSON: careful with integer serialization (no leading zeros, decimal).\n\nGiven the length, I'll produce the code.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to write a Python program `settle.py` that implements MUSUBI settle core v0 from spec. We have no reference output, only input files. Need to produce exactly output object.\n\nWe need to carefully read the spec and implement all the rules.\n\nKey challenges:\n- Implement canonical JSON, Ed25519 verification with cryptography, public key validation (point on curve, prime order, not identity), strict signature checks.\n- Parse JSON with duplicate key detection and depth constraint, and special handling of Python's json for NaN/Infinity etc. Need replicate behavior.\n- Contract verification (4.1) and settleable terms (4.2) and strict terms (4.3).\n- View verification (5.1) with Bitcoin header checks.\n- Record triage (6.0), binding (6.1), strictness, size limit, collapse identical signed body (6.4), authentication, conformance (6.6).\n- Anchors (7), event set and status (8).\n- Grant evaluation (10) with revocations, expiry, actions, approvals, delegation, forged approvals.\n- NENRIN checks (11).\n- Verdict and bond outcome (12, 9.3).\n\nNeed to output exactly JSON: contract_sha256, verdict, status, bond_outcome, deviation_codes, underspecified_reasons, event_set, dispositions.\n\nWe'll implement step by step.\n\nWe are allowed to use cryptography for Ed25519 only. We'll need to import `cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.ed25519` and possibly `cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization`. For public key validation, we need to check that the decoded point is on curve, not identity, and in prime-order subgroup? The spec says: \"A public key is also unusable if it is not the canonical encoding of a point on edwards25519, if it is the identity point, or if the point is not in the prime order subgroup (small order or mixed order points).\" In Ed25519, public keys are compressed Edwards points; we can use cryptography's `Ed25519PublicKey.from_public_bytes` but that doesn't check prime order subgroup beyond what OpenSSL does? Need to implement ourselves? We can do point decoding and check order. Spec says 3.2: \"public key is 32 bytes after decoding... A public key is also unusable if it is not the canonical encoding of a point on edwards25519, if it is the identity point, or if the point is not in the prime order subgroup\". So we need to decode compressed point, check it's on curve, not identity, and multiply by L to see if yields identity? Actually check that point is in prime-order subgroup. Since Ed25519 curve has cofactor 8, subgroup prime L. We can check by scalar multiplication by L (or by 8? Actually ensure that point is in subgroup of order L: multiply by L should give identity if point is in subgroup? For a point P, if P has order dividing L, then L*P = identity. But if P is a small order point, L*P = identity too? Actually if P has order divisible by L? Hmm. For Ed25519, all points are on curve, total order is 8*L. Subgroup of prime order L consists of points whose order divides L. To check if P is in prime-order subgroup, we can check that [L]P = identity (or [8]P != identity and [L]P = id? Actually for any point P, if P is in subgroup, its order divides L, so [L]P = id. If P is in cofactor subgroup (order 1,2,4,8), then [L]P = P? Not identity unless P is identity because L odd. So [L]P = id iff the point has order dividing L, i.e., point is in prime-order subgroup. So we can compute scalar multiplication by L and check identity. That requires bignum and curve math; we can use Python's cryptography? Not directly. We could use `cryptography` to verify a signature with this key and a known message? Not enough. Better implement Ed25519 point decompression and scalar multiplication modulo L? That's complex. Alternatively we can use `cryptography`'s `Ed25519PublicKey.from_public_bytes` which I think validates that the point is on curve and not identity? But does it check prime-order subgroup? I believe OpenSSL's Ed25519 verification does check that public key is in subgroup; but the spec requires we check separately for \"unusable\". We can use libsodium? Not allowed. We'll implement minimal Ed25519 public key validation using pure Python with big integer arithmetic.\n\nWe need to parse Ed25519 compressed points: 32 bytes, with sign bit in most significant bit of last byte; y coordinate is remaining 255 bits. Then x coordinate recovered from curve equation. Need check on curve, not identity, and prime-order subgroup via [L]P == identity.\n\nWe'll implement modular arithmetic. Python integers fine.\n\nDefine Ed25519 parameters:\np = 2**255 - 19\nd = -121665 * pow(121666, p-2, p) mod p? Actually d = -121665/121666 mod p.\nI = pow(2, (p-1)//4, p) for sqrt.\nL = 2**252 + 27742317777372353535851937790883648493.\n\nWe can either implement scalar multiplication or use formula for checking subgroup without full multiplication? Since we only need to check [L]P = id, we can compute =? For Edwards curve, scalar multiplication by L is heavy but doable with double-and-add. We'll implement Edwards point addition and doubling.\n\nNeed careful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps `cryptography`'s `Ed25519PublicKey.from_public_bytes` does check subgroup? The spec says reference uses cryptography (OpenSSL). OpenSSL likely rejects small order points for Ed25519? I'm not sure. But the spec says \"verify returns unusable when key or signature fails 3.2\", and 3.2 includes key point checks. So we must implement.\n\nWe'll do.\n\nFor signature strictness: check R point passes public key test (prime order, not identity) and S < L. R is first 32 bytes.\n\nNow implement canonical JSON. We need to replicate Python's json module for non-finite floats: `NaN`, `Infinity`, `-Infinity`, and finite floats with repr. We'll parse JSON using Python's json.loads with parse_float=decimal.Decimal? Better to keep floats? Spec says numbers must be integers within range for strictness. But we need to handle non-integer numbers for canonical serialization when signing bodies that may contain them (non-strict). So we need to preserve original number representation? But canonicalization uses whatever Python's json module would output for that number. Python's json module uses float repr for non-integer numbers, and outputs `NaN`, `Infinity`, `-Infinity` for those special values. When parsing JSON, Python's json module will parse `NaN` as float('nan'), `Infinity` as float('inf'), etc. So we can parse using json.loads with default parse_float=float. Then when we canonicalize, we need to convert Python objects to canonical text. For floats, we'll use Python's repr as specified (which matches json.dumps with allow_nan=True? Actually json.dumps outputs NaN, Infinity, -Infinity and repr for finite floats? Python's json.dumps uses float.__repr__ for finite floats? It uses `_make_iterencode` with floatstr=repr. So yes.)\n\nBut we need to detect non-integer numbers for strictness. Integers are those written as integer token (no fraction/exponent) and within range. We'll parse JSON with a custom decoder that can capture raw tokens? That's complex. Alternative: parse with Python's json, which will convert integer tokens to Python int, non-integer to float. We can distinguish int vs float. But need to detect exponent forms that produce integer value? E.g., `1e2` is float 100.0; not integer per spec. So if it's float, it's non-integer. However, Python's json may parse large integers as int if within range; fine. For numbers like `1e400` becomes inf (float). So that's non-integer. We need to enforce max integer token length 4300 digits; we can check raw text length? Alternatively, during parsing, we could use a custom parser that rejects integer tokens > 4300 digits. That's easier: before json.loads, we can scan the text? Might be simpler to implement a custom JSON parser that enforces deep nesting, duplicate keys, and returns Python objects while distinguishing integer tokens from floats. The spec says \"Text nested deeper than 512 levels ... error\". We can implement parser using recursion with depth counter, and handle duplicate keys, and detect numbers as either int (if integer token) or float (if has fraction/exponent or is NaN/Infinity). But we must replicate Python's json behavior for parsing `NaN`, `Infinity`, `-Infinity` as floats. We can just use Python's json.loads with object_pairs_hook to detect duplicates? Python's json.loads does not allow duplicate keys by default; it overwrites. We need to detect duplicate keys. We can use object_pairs_hook that gets list of pairs and check for duplicates. That will catch duplicates. For depth, Python's json has recursion limit? We can implement our own parser to control depth. Simpler: use json.loads with parse_float=float, parse_int=int, parse_constant? Actually parse_constant can catch NaN/Infinity, we treat as float. For integer token length > 4300, we can pre-scan text with regex to detect long integer tokens. But spec says \"integer token longer than 4300 digits is an error\". We can scan the raw text for sequences of digits that represent integer tokens, but need to avoid strings. We can implement a small state machine to find numbers outside strings. Alternatively, after parsing, we could detect if any int has more than 4300 digits? But Python can parse arbitrarily large ints, but the token length is what matters. If the token has leading zeros? Spec says integer tokens must have no leading zeros for canonical, but parse error? It doesn't explicitly forbid leading zeros in input, but integer tokens with leading zeros are not valid JSON? JSON spec doesn't allow leading zeros. Python's json will reject `01` as error. So we can rely on Python's json. For token length, we'll pre-scan using a simple scanner that ignores strings and counts digits in numbers that look like integer tokens (no '.', 'e', 'E').\n\nBut for simplicity, we may implement a lightweight JSON parser that handles depth, duplicates, and separates int/float. That's doable in about 200 lines.\n\nGiven the complexity, we'll write a custom JSON parser using a recursive descent parser with depth limit, converting strings, numbers, booleans, null, arrays, objects. It will be careful with numbers: parse integer if it matches integer format, else parse as float (including NaN, Infinity, -Infinity). It will raise ParseError for invalid JSON, long integer tokens, etc.\n\nWe'll also ensure that duplicate keys are detected: we'll accumulate keys in object and raise if duplicate.\n\nRegarding canonicalization, we need to sort object keys by Unicode code point. We'll use `sorted(obj.keys())`. For strings, escape control characters as specified.\n\nNow implement all steps.\n\nPlan outline:\n\n1. Read files from vector_dir: contract.json, view.json, events/*.json, nenrin/*.json, parent.json.\n   - All JSON text parse with our custom parser. If parse error, output error and exit 2.\n   - events: if directory missing, events=[]; else each file parse to value, append to list.\n   - nenrin: optional; if directory missing, absent (None). If present, treat as list; if parsed value is not list, treat as None (absent). Actually spec: \"`nenrin_records`: optional list ... A value that is not a list is treated as absent.\" So if the directory exists and files parse to a list? The input layout says each file holds one element of nenrin_records list. So we should combine all files into a list: read each file, parse, and append to list. If any file parse error? Then error. If the directory is missing, absent. If some file content is not list? It is one element, so it doesn't matter; we'll just put parsed values into a list. Since spec says nenrin_records is optional list of JSON values; if provided as vector, it's directory with each file holding one element. So we'll build list.\n\n2. Compute contract_sha256: Need canonical of contract without signatures key. If contract not object, contract_sha256 = null; else compute.\n   - Contract signing bytes = b\"a2a-contract-v0\\n\" + canonical(contract minus \"signatures\")\n   - sha256, hex.\n\n3. Execute main settle logic:\n   - We need to collect underspecified_reasons set and deviation_codes set.\n   - We also need dispositions mapping.\n   - We need to decide verdict, status, bond_outcome.\n\nWe'll follow the computation order as described but we must collect reasons without short-circuiting most checks (except when rules 4.1/4.2/5.1 rules 1-8 fail then no record examined further; but we still compute other non-record checks like 4.3, 5.1 rule 9, 6.0 on records? It says \"If a problem was found in 4.1, 4.2 or 5.1 rules 1 to 8, no record is examined further.\" So we skip record triage, collapse, etc. But we still may need to compute dispositions? No dispositions for unexamined records. But we still need to maybe output empty event_set and dispositions with only binding classes? The binding step (6.1) is part of record processing, but if we skip record examination, we might still need to compute binding classes? The spec says \"Binding runs even when sections 4 or 5 failed; its classes are then still reported.\" So we need to perform binding classification regardless of contract/view verification, but not triage. The order: compute contract_sha256, bind records (6.1). That is independent. Then if 4.1/4.2/5.1(1-8) have problems, no further record examination. So we must always compute binding classes for all events. So step: parse events list (must be list; if not list, reason input_not_settleable and stop? Actually if events not a list, settlement underspecified with input_not_settleable, and nothing else computed. So we handle that early: if events is not list, add reason and maybe output without further? Spec: \"If contract is not an object, the settlement is underspecified with the single reason contract_not_verified. If events is not a list, it is underspecified with input_not_settleable. Nothing else is computed in either case.\" So if events not list, we output error? Actually output settlement with reason input_not_settleable and maybe contract_sha256 etc. We'll set verdict=underspecified, reasons={input_not_settleable}, event_set empty, dispositions empty, status=undetermined, bond_outcome based on bond? Probably n/a or undetermined. We'll follow.\n\nBut we can still compute contract_sha256 if contract object.\n\nWe'll structure code with a main function that orchestrates.\n\nOverview of order:\n- Parse inputs. If any file missing? contract.json, view.json mandatory? Spec says view input one JSON object; if missing, error. We'll assume files exist. If parse error, print error and exit 2.\n- If events not list or contract not object, produce minimal output and exit 0? Actually on success prints output; those cases still produce settlement object, not error. So we should continue with underspecified reasons.\n- Compute contract_sha256 if contract is object; else null.\n- Perform binding on events (if events is list). Even if contract not object? If contract not object, can't compute csha, but binding maybe not needed? Spec says if contract not object only reason contract_not_verified, nothing else computed. So we skip binding.\n- Initialize sets: underspecified_reasons = set(), deviation_codes = set().\n- Process contract door (4.1) if contract is object. If any failure, add 'contract_not_verified' to reasons, and skip 4.2. Else run 4.2 checks adding reasons.\n- Process strict terms (4.3) regardless of 4.1 result: check contract_signature_not_strict, bond_malformed, parent_not_supplied, parent_narrowing_failed (requires 4.3.1).\n- Process view (5.1) if view exists. Even if contract not object? Spec: \"The view is checked even when section 4 already failed.\" So we should check view if contract is object? If contract not object, we may still check view? The spec says if contract not object, single reason contract_not_verified and nothing else computed. So maybe skip view. We'll follow spec strictly: if contract not object, stop after contract_not_verified. Same for events not list: stop after input_not_settleable. So we handle those early.\n- After contract and view checks, if there was a reason from 4.1 or 4.2 or 5.1 rules 1-8, we skip further record processing (triage etc). But we may still need to compute dispositions for binding? We already have binding classes from 6.1; we'll include those dispositions for bound records that are not dropped silently. But no further triage.\n\nWe need to be careful: the spec says \"If a problem was found in 4.1, 4.2 or 5.1 rules 1 to 8, no record is examined further.\" It does not say we stop binding; binding already done. So we should include dispositions for foreign/unbound/inconsistent even if we skip triage. However, if 4.1 failed, maybe we didn't compute contract_sha256? But we can compute csha from contract object regardless. So we can perform binding before or after contract door? The spec suggests computing csha and binding first (2.3). We'll do binding early.\n\nWe'll implement full flow in a class SettleCore.\n\nNow details of each section.\n\n4.1 Contract door: many rules. Need to implement all checks. We'll store reasons? Only contract_not_verified if any check fails; the specific refusal is informative. So we just need a boolean.\n\n- Rule 1: schema == \"a2a-contract-v0\".\n- Rule 2: contract_id and nonce are 32 lowercase hex.\n- Rule 3: parties list exactly two objects, roles exactly one principal and one contractor.\n- Rule 4: domain normalization: strip leading/trailing whitespace (Python str.strip()), lowercase, remove trailing dots, must satisfy pattern. Need at least two labels; each label regex ^[a-z0-9]([a-z0-9-]*[a-z0-9])?$ (so cannot start/end with hyphen, only alnum). Also no '/', '@', ':' or space. We'll implement.\n- Rule 5: key_url is https:// URL: parse with urllib.parse? Just check startswith \"https://\" and maybe valid? Spec says \"https:// URL\", likely any string starting with https://. We'll check scheme and maybe non-empty host? Could use urlparse and check scheme=='https' and netloc? But strict? We'll use urllib.parse.urlparse and check scheme=='https'.\n- Rule 6: public_key_ed25519_b64 decodes as canonical base64 to 32 bytes. We need to implement canonical base64 check: standard b64decode with validate=True, then re-encode with base64.b64encode and compare text. Also length 32.\n- Rule 7: task object with non-empty purpose; payload_digest if present not null must be 64 lower hex.\n- Rule 8: grant object, keys only allowed list.\n- Rule 9: authorized_actions and prohibited_actions lists of non-empty strings, disjoint.\n- Rule 10: typed keys checks as described. Many sub-checks.\n- Rule 11: no number anywhere in contract (including signatures) is non-integer, NaN, infinity, or outside ±2^53-1. We'll recursively traverse contract value and check each number is int and within range.\n- Rule 12: every object key anywhere printable ASCII (32-126). Recursively check.\n- Rule 13: establishes non-empty list; no string matches case-insensitive regex patterns. We'll implement regex matching with re.search and IGNORECASE.\n- Rule 14: does_not_establish non-empty list; lowercased strings together contain four groups. Need to combine all lowercased strings maybe with spaces? Spec: \"Lowercased, its strings together contain: one of runtime or enforce; one of judge, liability or fault; deviat; and legal.\" To check, we can join all strings lowercased with space and then check each group. Or check each group across any string. We'll join with ' ' and use regex.\n- Rule 15: signatures list; for each object, domain normalizes to a party's domain; its signature verified with party key over contract signing bytes. False or unusable refuses, even if same party has good signature later. So we check all signatures, if any bad, fail. Need party key from parties by domain.\n- Rule 16: each party has at least one signature that verifies.\n\nWe'll implement a function verifies_signature(pub_b64, sig_b64, msg) returning bool or unusable. We'll treat unusable as false for refusal.\n\nFor public key validation, we need to decode and check point. We'll implement `decode_public_key(pub_b64)` returning point or raises.\n\nWhen verifying, we need to handle strictness? The door just needs verify (3.4) which returns true/false/unusable; strictness not relevant here. So we use cryptography's verify for Ed25519. But we must also check key validity per 3.2 (point checks) before using. So we'll implement `verify_ed25519(pub_bytes, sig_bytes, message)` that first validates public key, then attempts verification using cryptography. If signature length !=64, unusable. If invalid, false.\n\nNote: Cryptography's `Ed25519PublicKey.from_public_bytes(pub_bytes).verify(sig, msg)` will raise InvalidSignature if fails. We'll catch and return false.\n\n4.2 Settleable terms: only if 4.1 accepts. We'll implement checks adding reasons.\n\n4.3 Strict terms: always run. Checks:\n- contract_signature_not_strict: for each signatures entry, if sig decodes to 64 bytes and not strict (S < L and R point valid). If any non-strict, add reason.\n- bond_malformed: if bond present not null, and not object with amount integer 1..2^53-1. Add reason.\n- parent_not_supplied: if parent_contract present not null and parent not supplied. Add reason.\n- parent_narrowing_failed: if parent_contract present not null, and parent provided but fails 4.3.1. Add reason.\n\n4.3.1 Parent narrowing (grant_subset): check many conditions between child grant and parent grant. Need to follow exactly. We'll implement.\n\nView verification (5.1) and 5.2 target decoding.\n\nWe'll have a Bitcoin header verification for a list of headers. We'll parse raw bytes from hex. Check height integers, hex length 160 (or 161 with newline? rule 9 handles strictness separately; for rules 1-8 we only need 160 hex, but if newline included, the hex string length >160 maybe. Actually rule 4 says each header hex 160 hex chars optionally followed by one line feed. In rule 1-8, we treat hex as 160 chars? We can strip trailing '\\n' for verification? Rule 4 says optionally followed by one line feed. So for chain verification, we can accept if after removing one trailing '\\n' it's 160 hex. We'll implement that for rules 1-8.\n- Rule 1: grant.finality.max_target_bits must decode to target; store floor target.\n- Rule 2: lower_bound object with integer height and 64 lower hex hash.\n- Rule 3: headers non-empty.\n- Rule 4: each header object with integer height >=0 and hex (after possibly stripping one newline) 160 hex, compute raw bytes.\n- Rule 5: heights consecutive.\n- Rule 6: from second header, prev hash matches bytes 4..36 reversed.\n- Rule 7: nBits decode, target <= floor, header hash <= target.\n- Rule 8: view holds header at lower_bound.height with hash equal to lower_bound.hash.\nIf any fails, add chain_view_rejected.\n\nRule 9: strictness of view: if view object with headers list, every header object with integer height and hex exactly 160 lower hex (no newline), and every string/key/number strict. If not, add view_not_strict. This is separate and does not stop walk.\n\nIf view verifies but >200000 headers, add input_too_large instead of chain_view_rejected? Spec: \"If the view verifies but holds more than 200000 headers, the reason is input_too_large instead, and no record is examined.\" So if rules 1-8 pass and headers count >200000, add input_too_large, and skip record examination. Need careful: We'll check after rules 1-8: if no chain_view_rejected and headers >200000, add input_too_large and mark skip_records.\n\nNow record handling flow:\n\nWe'll have a list of events (parsed from files). We need to produce for each event:\n- record_sha256 (if computable)\n- binding class (foreign, inconsistent, unbound, bound, dropped silently)\n- triage result (kept, set aside with reason, rejected, etc.)\n- for kept records: collapse, conformance, anchors, event_set.\n\nWe'll implement step by step in a single pass to manage state.\n\nState variables:\n- events: list\n- contract: object or None\n- csha: contract_sha256 or None\n- cid: contract.contract_id if object\n- pdg: contract.task.payload_digest (if present) else None\n- records_by_sha: dict to deduplicate identical bytes (record_sha256). We'll store first occurrence.\n- records: list of record objects that are bound and pass initial triage? We'll maintain a list of all records after binding and before triage.\n\nSince we might skip triage if contract/view failure, we need to still assign dispositions for binding classes. We'll have a function `classify_binding(record)` that returns (class, reason) and maybe store in dispositions.\n\nThe spec says \"Skip a record whose record_sha256 was already seen (identical bytes are one record).\" So we need to compute record_sha256 for each event. This requires canonical(record) which may fail for non-finite numbers? Actually canonical of any JSON value works; but if integer token too long, we may have parsed but not know; our parser will reject long integers entirely, so no issue. However, if numbers are float NaN etc, canonical can compute. So record_sha256 should be computable for all object records. For non-object records? The spec says \"Skip anything that is not an object, or whose schema is not one of three\". So we don't compute sha for those; no disposition.\n\nSo we loop over events in order:\n- if not object: skip.\n- if schema not one of three: skip.\n- compute record_sha256 (hex of sha256 of canonical(record)).\n- if sha already seen: skip (no new record).\n- else compute binding class using 6.1 rules.\n- store record info: record, sha, binding_class.\n- If class is \"bound\", add to bound_records list; else add to dispositions mapping with class.\n- Foreign/inconsistent: no triage.\n- Unbound: not settled in strict mode, but no triage? Spec says \"Only bound records go further. Foreign, inconsistent and unbound records are listed (section 9) and never settled.\" So unbound gets disposition \"unbound\" but no further processing.\n- Bound: will go further if we decide to process records (i.e., no skip due to contract/view).\n\nNote: \"not ours\" dropped silently, no disposition. That's when contract_ref absent/null and ref.contract_id != cid. So class \"not ours\" => no disposition; we just ignore.\n\nAfter binding, we have a list of bound records (with sha). If skip_records due to contract/view/large input/input_not_settleable etc, we don't go further; but we still have dispositions for foreign/inconsistent/unbound. Bound records get no disposition because unexamined.\n\nIf we proceed to triage (6.0), we need to process each bound record.\n\nTriage table (6.0). For each bound record:\n- Compute body: record without signatures and anchor.\n- Compute body strictness: as per strict definition, including specific checks (nenrin_ref, contract_ref fields, performed_actions type, delegated_to type, approvals list and sig strictness). The spec defines \"body is not strict when ...\" We'll implement `is_body_strict(record)`.\n- Compute anchor strictness (if anchor present).\n- Determine strictly signed: need one signature entry with allowed role and strictly verifies with matching key and is itself strict value. We'll implement `is_strictly_signed(record)` returning bool.\n- Then apply table in order:\n  - if not strictly signed and body not strict: set aside, reason record_not_strict? Actually table first row: \"not strictly signed, body not strict | set aside, record_not_strict\". So set aside, disposition \"record_not_strict\", no reason added? Wait \"The first three set aside or reject without one\". The first row says set aside, record_not_strict but no underspecified reason? The text: \"Only the fourth row both sets a record aside and adds a reason; the first three set aside or reject without one\". So first three do not add to underspecified_reasons. So we just set disposition accordingly (record_not_strict or signature_not_strict or rejected) but no reason.\n  - second: not strictly signed but signature verifies under 3.4 alone (lax) -> set aside, signature_not_strict, no reason.\n  - third: not strictly signed otherwise -> rejected, no reason.\n  - fourth: strictly signed, body not strict -> set aside, record_not_strict AND add reason record_not_strict.\n  - fifth: strictly signed, anchor not strict, and another bound record with same record signing bytes kept and anchors validly -> set aside, anchor_not_strict (no reason? The table says \"set aside, anchor_not_strict\" and this is one of first three? Actually the first three are rows 1-3; rows 4-6 are different. Row 5 says set aside, anchor_not_strict, but does it add reason? The paragraph says: \"Only the fourth row both sets a record aside and adds a reason; the first three set aside or reject without one\". So row 5 doesn't add reason? But the disposition is anchor_not_strict. However, row 6: strictly signed, anchor not strict, otherwise -> underspecified reason anchor_not_strict. So row 5 is when there is another valid copy with same signing bytes, so no reason; row 6 when no other valid copy, add reason. We'll implement accordingly.\n  - seventh: revocation and grant.revocation not object -> set aside, revocation_not_provided_for, no reason.\n  - else kept.\n\nWe need to track for each record: signed_body_bytes = record signing bytes (canonical of body? Actually record signing bytes are context + canonical(record without signatures and anchor). That's body canonical plus schema-specific prefix. We'll compute that for grouping later. However, for strict signed check we need verify signature over those bytes. We'll compute record_signing_bytes = context + canonical(body) (where body = record without signatures and anchor). Note: body includes contract_ref etc.\n\nWe'll store for each bound record: record_sha, record, body_is_strict, anchor_is_strict, strictly_signed, signed_body_bytes, maybe signatures info.\n\nAfter triage, we keep only records with result \"kept\". We'll have a list `kept_records`.\n\nSize limit: if len(kept_records) > 10000? Actually \"If more than 10000 records are kept by 6.0, add input_too_large and no record examined further.\" So if count > 10000, add reason input_too_large and skip further processing. We should add to underspecified_reasons; and no further examination. But we already have dispositions for triaged records (the set aside/rejected). Those remain. Bound records that were kept but not examined further? They get no disposition because unexamined? The spec says \"no record is examined further.\" So we stop before collapse/conformance/anchor. We'll not run those steps. The kept_records list will not get further dispositions.\n\nIf not too large, proceed to collapse (6.4) but only if section 5 rules 1-8 passed. Actually collapse is done when section 5 rules 1-8 passed, whether or not section 4 did. So we need to know if chain_view_rejected was added. If yes, skip collapse. If no, perform collapse.\n\nCollapse: group kept records by record_signing_bytes. For each group with more than one distinct record_sha256:\n- Determine which copies anchor validly (if any). Anchor validly: anchor.height integer >=0 that view holds; block_hash equals view hash; proof valid; batch leg accepts.\n- If at least one valid, keep the one with smallest (height, record_sha256) and drop others as duplicate_dropped.\n- If none valid, keep all.\n\nThis needed view information